Topological sorting is a linear ordering of the vertices of a directed acyclic graph (DAG) in which every vertex appears before all vertices to which it has an outgoing edge.
Algorithm — Kahn's Method
1. Calculate the indegree of every vertex.
2. Insert all vertices with indegree zero into a queue.
3. Remove a vertex from the queue and print it.
4. Decrease the indegree of its adjacent vertices.
5. Insert any vertex whose indegree becomes zero into the queue.
6. Repeat until the queue is empty.
C program
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int a[10][10], indeg[10] = {0};
    int q[10], n, front = 0, rear = 0;
    int count = 0, u, v;

    scanf("%d", &n);

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
            scanf("%d", &a[i][j]);

            if (a[i][j])
                indeg[j]++;
        }

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        if (indeg[i] == 0)
            q[rear++] = i;

    while (front < rear) {
        u = q[front++];
        printf("%d ", u);
        count++;

        for (v = 0; v < n; v++) {
            if (a[u][v]) {
                indeg[v]--;

                if (indeg[v] == 0)
                    q[rear++] = v;
            }
        }
    }

    if (count != n)
        printf("\nGraph contains a cycle");

    return 0;
}

Example
Suppose the directed edges are:
\[
A\rightarrow C,\quad B\rightarrow C,\quad
C\rightarrow D
\]
A valid topological ordering is:
\[
\boxed{A,B,C,D}
\]
Another valid ordering is:
\[
\boxed{B,A,C,D}
\]
There can be multiple valid topological orderings.

